 <u> (Without Reference to File) </u> 

CONCURRENCE IN SENATE AMENDMENTS
AB 1 X1 (Villaraigosa)
As Amended March 18, 1999
Majority vote
 <u> 
ASSEMBLY: 47-24                 (February 25, 1999)              
SENATE:   23-8 (March 22, 1999)__________
 </u> <u> 
 </u> Original Committee Reference:   <u> ED. </u> 

 <u> SUMMARY </u> :  Establishes the California Peer Assistance and Review  
(PAR) Program for Teachers for the purpose of assisting veteran  
teachers in need of development in subject matter knowledge or  
teaching strategies.

 <u> The Senate amendments </u> :

1)Sunset the California Mentor Teacher Program July 1, 2001 and  
  establish in its place the PAR program, which would become  
  fully operational on the same date.


2)Delete the provisions requiring the Superintendent of Public  
  Instruction (SPI) to develop and the State Board of Education  
  to approve guidelines for the local implementation of a PAR  
  program.


3)Specify that PAR programs shall expect and strongly encourage  
  a cooperative relationship between the consulting teacher and  
  the principal with respect to the process of peer assistance  
  and review.


4)State legislative intent that school districts be allowed to  
  combine programs of peer assistance and review by mutual  
  agreement.


5)Specify that not more than 5% of the funds received by a  
  school district for the PAR program may be expended for  
  administrative expenses.









                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  2


6)Specify that a district that elects not to participate in the  
  PAR program by July 1, 2001 is not eligible for specified  
  "Mega-Item" funding in the Budget Act or funding for the  
  Administrator Training and Evaluation Program.


7)Provide that a district that receives funding for the PAR  
  program may also expend the funds for the California  
  Pre-Internship Teaching Program, a district intern program,  
  professional development, or any program that supports the  
  training and development of new teachers.


8)Provide that districts that participate in the PAR program by  
  June 30, 2000 receive funding equal to the number of mentor  
  teachers they received funding for in the 1999-2000 fiscal  
  year, multiplied by $2,800.  Districts that participate after  
  July 1, 2000 but before May 31, 2001 may receive funding equal  
  to the number of mentor teachers funded in 1999-2000  
  multiplied by $1,000.  These funds may be used for activities  
  necessary to implement the PAR program.


9)Require, in districts participating in the PAR program, any  
  certificated employee who receives an unsatisfactory rating on  
  an evaluation to participate in the PAR program.


 <u> EXISTING LAW </u> :

1)Establishes the California Mentor Teacher Program and provides  
  that the primary function of a mentor teacher is to provide  
  assistance and guidance to new teachers.  Mentor teachers may  
  provide staff development for teachers and develop special  
  curriculum. 


2)Requires the governing board of each school district to  
  evaluate and assess certificated employee performance as it  
  reasonably relates to the progress of pupils toward the  
  standards of expected pupil achievement established by the  
  governing board.










                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  3

 <u> AS PASSED BY THE ASSEMBLY </u> ,  this bill:

1)Sunsetted the California Mentor Teacher Program July 1, 2000  
  and established in its place the PAR  program, which would  
  become fully operational on the same date.


2)Specified that a school district that accepts state funds for  
  this program agrees to negotiate the development and  
  implementation of the program with the exclusive  
  representative of the certificated employees in the school  
  district, if there is an exclusive representative of the  
  certificated employees.


3)Specified that a school district that elects not to  
  participate in this program is not eligible for funding for  
  PAR or for:  a) the Beginning Teacher Support and Assessment  
  (BTSA) program; b) the Instructional Time and Staff  
  Development Reform program; and, c) school development plans.   
  Also required districts not participating in this program to  
  report annually at a regularly scheduled meeting of the school  
  district governing board on the rationale for not  
  participating in the program.


4)Specified that a district that receives funding for  
  participating in the PAR program may also use the funding for  
  BTSA and the employment of district interns.


5)Required, at a minimum, the following principles to be  
  included in locally developed peer review programs:


   a)   Teacher participants in the program who have permanent  
     status and volunteer to participate or be referred for  
     participation in the program as a result of their biennial  
     evaluation; 

   b)   Written and clearly stated performance goals for  
     individual teachers that are aligned with pupil learning  
     goals;

   c)   Review and assistance that includes multiple  








                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  4

     observations of a teacher during periods of classroom  
     instruction;

   d)   Sufficient staff development activities to assist  
     teachers to improve their teaching skills and knowledge;

   e)   A monitoring component with a written record; and, 



   f)   The final evaluation of a teacher's participation in the  
     program must be made available for placement in the  
     teacher's personnel file.


6)Required a mentor teacher participating in this program to:   
  a) be a credentialed classroom teacher with permanent status;  
  b) have substantial recent experience in classroom  
  instruction; and, c) have demonstrated exemplary teaching  
  ability. "Exemplary teaching ability" is indicated by  
  effective communication skills, subject matter knowledge, and  
  mastery of a range of teaching strategies.


7)Required a joint teacher administrator peer review panel to be  
  established by participating districts to govern the PAR  
  program. The panel must select mentors for the program, review  
  peer review reports, and make recommendations to the school  
  district governing board regarding program participants. This  
  duty would include forwarding to the governing board the names  
  of individuals who are not able to demonstrate satisfactory  
  improvement after getting assistance.


8)Required the joint peer review panel to annually evaluate the  
  impact of the PAR program. The panel may submit  
  recommendations for improvement of the program to the district  
  governing board and to the exclusive representative of the  
  certificated employees of the district, if there is an  
  exclusive representative of the certificated employees.


9)Required school district governing boards to evaluate and  
  assess certificated employee performance as it reasonably  
  relates to the progress of pupils toward the state-adopted  








                                                         <u> AB 1 X1
                                                         </u> Page  5

  academic content standards as measured by state-adopted  
  criterion referenced assessments.  Also authorized a school  
  district to require that a certificated employee who receives  
  an unsatisfactory rating in this evaluation to participate in  
  its Mentor Peer Review and Assistance program.


10)Required SPI, subject to the availability of funding in the  
  annual Budget Act, to contract with an independent evaluator  
  on or before December 15, 2002 to prepare a comprehensive  
  evaluation of the implementation, impact, cost, and benefit of  
  the PAR program.  This evaluation would be required to be  
  delivered to the Legislature, the Governor, and interested  
  parties on or before January 1, 2004.


 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :   Appropriates $125 million (Proposition 98) from  
the General Fund (GF) for the 1999-2000 fiscal year, with $41.8  
million for the PAR program and $83.2 million for the California  
Mentor Program.  This bill also appropriates from GF $82,000  
(Non-Proposition 98) to SPI to provide support services related  
to this program.

 <u> COMMENTS </u> : This bill is one of four bills sponsored by the  
Governor for the special legislative session on education. The  
other bills are AB 2X (Mazzoni), which would establish the  
Elementary School Intensive Reading Program, SB 1X (Alpert),  
which would establish the Public School Performance  
Accountability Program, and SB 2X (O'Connell), which would  
create a high school exit examination.

